# Project Flour Global Rule

## 1. Technology Stack

Project Flour is a production-grade web platform.

Technology stack:

- Next.js (App Router)
- React
- TypeScript (strict mode)
- Tailwind CSS
- Supabase
- PostgreSQL
- GitHub
- Vercel
- Mobile-first responsive design

Do not introduce additional frameworks or libraries unless explicitly approved.

---

## 2. Coding Standards

- TypeScript strict mode.
- Reusable components.
- Clean architecture.
- Small focused files.
- Strong typing.
- Meaningful naming.
- Avoid duplicated logic.
- Prefer composition over large monolithic components.
- Prefer server-side validation for business logic.
- Never hardcode configurable business rules.
- Code should be production-ready rather than prototype quality.

---

## 3. Terminology

Official product name:

Whitebird Operating System (WOS)

Internal codename:

Project Flour

Official workspace names:

- Home
- Customer Operations
- Bakery
- Collection
- Management
- Owner

Never replace these names with generic software terminology.

Use "Bakery", never "Kitchen".

---

## 4. Architecture Philosophy

Project Flour follows a layered architecture.

Foundation Layer

- Authentication
- Users
- Roles
- Audit
- Timeline

Core Engine Layer

- Business Rules Engine
- Business Calendar Engine
- Menu Engine
- Capacity Engine
- Order Validation Engine
- Task Engine
- Responsibility Engine
- Suggestion Engine

Workspace Layer

- Home
- Customer Operations
- Bakery
- Collection
- Management
- Owner

Future modules must extend this architecture instead of replacing it.

---

## 5. Implementation Principles

Implement one milestone at a time.

Every milestone must:

- compile successfully
- be deployable
- be independently testable

Never redesign previous milestones unless explicitly requested.

Prefer incremental implementation.

Do not generate speculative features.

If requirements are unclear, ask.

---

## 6. Business Rules Philosophy

Business rules belong to the Business Rules Engine.

Business rules must not be duplicated throughout the application.

Whenever practical, business rules should be configurable instead of hardcoded.

Examples:

- pickup windows
- operating hours
- business calendar
- capacity
- seasonal availability
- order cutoff

Operational decisions remain human decisions.

The system may recommend actions but must never automatically perform business decisions unless explicitly requested.

---

## 7. Do's

- Build maintainable code.
- Use reusable components.
- Respect existing architecture.
- Keep business logic separate from UI.
- Keep database migrations reversible.
- Use meaningful commit structure.
- Ask questions instead of inventing requirements.

---

## 8. Don'ts

Do not:

- redesign architecture
- rename agreed terminology
- introduce unnecessary dependencies
- hardcode business rules
- create generic dashboard templates
- generate fake placeholder business logic
- silently change database schema
- implement automatic business decisions

If unsure, stop and ask.

---

## Project Philosophy

Project Flour is not a generic bakery POS.

It is a long-term operational platform for Whitebird Cake House.

The objective is to mirror real business operations rather than generic software patterns.

Architecture decisions always take precedence over implementation convenience.
